.TH MISC.H 3v32 "October 11, 2026" "Vircon32 DevTools 26.04.24" "Vircon32 C Library"
.SH NAME
misc.h \- Vircon32 memory operations, dynamic memory, random numbers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include \(dqmisc.h\(dq
.PP
.BI "void  memset( void* " destination ", int " value ", int " size " );"
.BI "void  memcpy( void* " destination ", void* " source ", int " size " );"
.BI "int   memcmp( void* " region1 ", void* " region2 ", int " size " );"
.PP
.BI "void* malloc( int " size " );"
.BI "void* calloc( int " number ", int " size " );"
.BI "void* realloc( void* " ptr ", int " size " );"
.BI "void  free( void* " ptr " );"
.PP
.B "void* malloc_start_address;"
.B "void* malloc_end_address;"
.PP
.B "int   rand();"
.BI "void  srand( int " seed " );"
.PP
.B "void  exit();"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
All sizes are in words (the unit of
.BR sizeof ).
.SS Memory
.BR memset ,
.B memcpy
and
.B memcmp
are each a single string instruction of the CPU, processing one word
per cycle.
.TP
.B memset
Set
.I size
words at
.I destination
to
.IR value .
Unlike C's, it fills whole words and returns nothing.
.TP
.B memcpy
Copy
.I size
words from
.I source
to
.IR destination ,
first to last; returns nothing.
.TP
.B memcmp
Compare
.I size
words, returning 0 if they are all equal, or else the first difference
.RI ( region1 " word minus " region2
word).
.SS Dynamic memory
A first-fit allocator over a block of RAM, from
.B malloc_start_address
to
.B malloc_end_address
(both included). By default these are words 1048576 to 3145727, the
middle half of RAM: the first quarter is left for global variables and
the last for the stack. A program may change them before its first call
to
.BR malloc ;
later changes have no effect.
.TP
.B malloc
Allocate
.I size
words. Returns
.B NULL
(\-1 on Vircon32) when there is no free block large enough, or when
.I size
is 0 or less.
.TP
.B calloc
Allocate
.IR number " \(mu " size
words, set to 0.
.TP
.B realloc
Resize a block, in place when it can, otherwise by allocating a new
block, copying and freeing the old one. With a
.B NULL
.I ptr
it acts as
.BR malloc ;
with a
.I size
of 0 or less it frees the block and returns
.BR NULL .
If a new block is needed and cannot be allocated, it returns
.B NULL
and the old block stays valid.
.TP
.B free
Release a block. Freeing
.B NULL
does nothing; freeing anything else that did not come from these
functions corrupts the allocator.
.SS Random numbers
.TP
.B rand
The next value of the console's random number generator, from 1 to
2147483646 (a Lehmer generator, the same as C++11's
.BR minstd_rand ).
.TP
.B srand
Seed the generator: only the low 31 bits of
.I seed
are kept, and the next
.B rand
returns that value itself before the sequence moves on. A seed of 0 is
ignored. The console is seeded with 1 at power-on and on reset, so a
program that never seeds it gets the same sequence every time; seeding
from
.BR get_time (3v32)
or from the frame on which the player pressed Start varies it.
.SS Program flow
.TP
.B exit
Halt the CPU (the
.B HLT
instruction). The screen and sound stay as they are; only a reset
restarts the program. It takes no status: Vircon32 programs have no
exit codes.
.SH NOTES
.BR memset ,
.BR memcpy ,
.BR malloc ,
.BR calloc ,
.BR realloc ,
.BR free ,
.BR rand ,
.B srand
and
.B exit
share their names with standard C functions but differ from them as
described above. For C programs transpiled by
.BR v32c++ (1),
its C library provides standard versions of the allocator functions
and of
.B exit
(see
.BR v32libc (3v32)).
.PP
A seed whose low 31 bits are all 0 or all 1, such as
.B INT_MIN
or \-1, makes
.B rand
return 0 from then on.
.PP
The allocator keeps a 4-word header before each block.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR vircon32 (3v32),
.BR time.h (3v32),
.BR misc.hpp (3v32)
.SH AUTHOR
The Vircon32 standard library was written by Javier Carracedo
.RI ( Carra ).
This manual page was written by Matthew Haas for the v32c++ project.
